    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-11373",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14158590/assets/95bb2c41fc54db270b8f6c884767868a8c691372a5d0910109f67582ef361ac6.png)\n\n \n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=10x$ for $y \\leq 0$ and $z \\leq 0,$ shown above.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}} \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\cos\\phi) \\:  \\rho^2\\sin\\phi \\: \\mathrm{d}\\rho \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta \\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $5$ centered at $(5,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x^2+y^2+z^2=10x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =10x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =10\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =10\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14158590/assets/53c68b2181ffbc7bd4a47206f67ee3da2af19d5f80683fd5ecb367c7d57bd47a.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq \\rho \\leq 10\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\:\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi , \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\rho }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\rho }\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14158590/observations/step-e11373-0d12a0eb4d08f1040a01.html",
